<div id="2026-11-12-decimal-scale-for-rounding" class="cl-entry">
<h3>2026-11-12 — Four inventory decimal columns stored less precision than the code computes</h3>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>What.</strong> <code>InventoryItem</code> now declares <code>unit_cost</code> <code>decimal(12,4)</code>, <code>markup_percent</code> <code>decimal(8,4)</code>, <code>discount_percent</code> <code>decimal(8,4)</code> and <code>net_weight_g</code> <code>decimal(10,3)</code>. Previously <code>(10,2)</code>, <code>(6,2)</code>, <code>(10,0)</code> and <code>(10,2)</code>, while the tables held <code>(10,0)</code> for <code>markup_percent</code> and <code>net_weight_g</code>. The database side still needs <em>Update Table</em> in schema comparison before the columns match the Result declarations.</p>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Why.</strong> Storage scale was narrower than working precision, which loses data on write and then compounds: <code>Util::Manufacturing::Costing</code> computes unit cost to four decimals (<code>sprintf '%.4f'</code>) and <code>moving_average()</code> feeds the <em>stored</em> value back in on every receipt, so a 2-decimal column rounds the cost again on each receipt. A markup solved from cost and price is 33.3333, and <code>ceil_to()</code> then prices from a truncated percentage, under-pricing silently. <code>discount_percent</code> at <code>(10,0)</code> could not hold 12.5% at all. <code>net_weight_g</code> is a divisor in <code>HealthKitchen::consume_recipe</code> and 1 lb = 453.592 g, while stock quantities are held at three decimals (<code>inventory_stock_levels.quantity_on_hand</code> <code>decimal(12,3)</code>).</p>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Rule.</strong> Storage scale must be at least the scale the code computes in; display rounds separately. Never fit the Result class to whatever the table happens to hold, and never widen a column that is already right — <code>unit_price</code> and <code>selling_price</code> stay <code>(10,2)</code> because <code>ceil_to()</code> already rounds them to a 0.05 step.</p>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Files.</strong> <code>lib/Comserv/Model/Schema/Ency/Result/Accounting/InventoryItem.pm</code>; <code>t/schema_result_parser_array_size.t</code> (now pins the four scales and fails if one is narrowed below the code's working precision). Seen on schema comparison as <code>markup_percent</code> table <code>(10,0)</code> vs Result <code>(6,2)</code> while applying <code>inventory_items.net_weight_g</code> for Health Kitchen recipe deductions.</p>
</div>
